**Issue**: #286
**Date**: 2026-08-27
**Status**: Approved
**Author**: NMG

@issue-286 @enhancement @verification @process-supervision
Feature: Remove wall-clock timeboxing from SDLC execution
  As a developer running nmg-sdlc workflows
  I want healthy work supervised by process state rather than elapsed time
  So that long-running delivery and verification reach their real outcome

  Scenario: Missing timeout means no deadline
    Given a valid steering validation omits "timeoutMs"
    When the steering runtime loads and executes it
    Then validation succeeds
    And no finite deadline is attached to the provider request

  Scenario: Managed validation descriptors omit timeout fields
    Given nmg-sdlc generates or upgrades managed steering
    When validation descriptors and canonical commands are written
    Then they contain no "timeoutMs" field
    And they contain no "--timeout-ms" or "--max-time" deadline flag
    And a second generation is byte-identical

  Scenario: Healthy command outlives a former deadline
    Given a built-in validation command remains alive and healthy
    And it completes successfully after a former finite limit
    When verification supervises the command
    Then the command is not killed because time elapsed
    And its result is passed

  Scenario: Healthy extension provider outlives a former deadline
    Given an extension provider remains pending before returning valid evidence
    When verification invokes the provider
    Then no timer races the provider
    And its eventual valid result is preserved

  Scenario: Active command is explicitly cancelled
    Given a validation command owns a child process group
    When the caller aborts its cancellation signal
    Then the owned process group is terminated exactly once
    And the result is incomplete with stable cancelled evidence
    And unrelated processes remain untouched

  Scenario: Exercise process is explicitly cancelled
    Given the OMP RPC exercise child is alive
    When the CLI receives explicit cancellation
    Then the owned process group is cleaned up
    And the exercise exits as cancelled rather than timed out

  Scenario Outline: Cleanup is cross-platform and bounded to owned work
    Given an active owned child on <platform>
    When cancellation or confirmed process loss requires cleanup
    Then <cleanup> targets only that child group or tree
    And an already-exited child is tolerated

    Examples:
      | platform | cleanup                              |
      | POSIX    | a negative owned process-group pid   |
      | Windows  | taskkill with the owned pid and tree |

  Scenario: Child disappears without terminal status
    Given a supervised child has not produced a valid terminal result
    When the runtime observes closure without an exit code or signal
    Then it classifies confirmed process loss
    And it does not wait forever or report success

  Scenario: Herdr worker remains active without a deadline
    Given an execute worker remains observable and working
    When the controller waits for settlement
    Then every Herdr wait omits a timeout
    And elapsed time and poll count do not end the wait

  Scenario: Herdr worker loss fails closed
    Given an execute worker disappears before a valid handoff
    When the controller confirms the pane or process is lost
    Then execution stops with a stable process-loss reason
    And no later issue or lifecycle step starts

  Scenario: Review and CI polling remains unbounded
    Given a PR check or eligible automated review remains pending
    When delivery continues observing it
    Then no elapsed-time or poll-count ceiling ends observation
    And observation ends only on success, genuine failure, explicit cancellation, or confirmed process loss

  Scenario Outline: Existing fail-closed verification remains strict
    Given an applicable required validation produces <condition>
    When verification aggregates results
    Then the overall status is not Pass
    And evidence identity and coverage rules remain enforced

    Examples:
      | condition            |
      | a nonzero exit       |
      | a launch failure     |
      | malformed evidence   |
      | stale identity       |
      | explicit cancellation|
      | confirmed process loss |

  Scenario: Published release is installed and reported
    Given the exact verified issue branch is merged and released
    When the released plugin is installed into the active OMP installation
    Then a fresh installed-plugin query reports that released version
    And the release, merge, issue, and PR evidence identify the same change
